  [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"## TL;DR\nThe a8w4 MoE gather-index `tt.load` produces **8 `v_readfirstlane_b32` per K-loop iteration** (lifting the gather row-indices VGPR→SGPR for the TDM gather operand). They are not hoistable by LLVM. We found two orthogonal causes and prototyped fixes:\n- **Vectorization** (kernel-side): `16× global_load_u16 + 8 pack → 2× cluster_load_b128`. Does **not** remove the readfirstlanes.\n- **Scalar load** (compiler-side, gated prototype): load the wave-uniform indices via `s_load` directly into SGPRs → **in-loop readfirstlane 8 → 0**.\n\nAll numbers below are produced on Triton **`upstream/main` (`9c795a41fc`, 2026-06-24)** — the open-source `github.com/triton-lang/triton`, LLVM pin `62b7cf96`.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Problem definition\n\n### Kernel / repro\n- **Repo:** `https://github.com/ROCm/aiter`\n- **Branch:** `moe_a8w4_multicast`\n- **Commit:** `3539d32ea71dbfd5b537013f1751b88914e0ca98` (\"configure cta layout for prefill and decode\")\n- **Kernel:** `aiter/ops/triton/_gluon_kernels/gfx1250/moe/moe_op_gemm_a8w4.py` → `_moe_gemm_a8w4_prefill`\n- **Triton:** `https://github.com/triton-lang/triton` **`upstream/main` @ `9c795a41fc`** (2026-06-24, LLVM pin `62b7cf96`). Everything in this ticket — baseline diagnosis, the vectorization patch, and the s_load patch — was built and run on this commit. The kernel needs upstream commit `9499e261e4` (\"[AMD][gfx1250] TDM: make async_tdm_gather/scatter pure\", #10685, 2026-06-19) for the `async_gather(desc, src_row_indices, dst, …)` signature; that commit is on `upstream/main`.\n- **Run** (gfx1250 FFM-lite simulator; `AITER_TRITON_ONLY=1` skips the CK/HIP build, `PYTHONPATH` because aiter isn't pip-installed):\n  ```bash\n  source <AM-r6.06>/ffmlite_env.sh\n  cd aiter/aiter/ops/triton/moe\n  AITER_TRITON_ONLY=1 PYTHONPATH=<aiter-root> TRITON_ALWAYS_COMPILE=1 \\\n      python3 moe_op_gemm_a8w4.py --M 1024\n  ```\n  (`--M 1024` → prefill: `block_m=128, block_n=256, block_k=256, num_warps=4, num_buffers=3, num_ctas=4`.)\n\n### The in-loop readfirstlane (baseline asm)\nThe gather-index load `offs_x_m = gl.load(GatherIndx + offs_x_m)` (kernel line ~1006) feeds the TDM gather's **scalar** row-index operand. The indices arrive in VGPRs and are lifted to SGPRs **inside the K-loop, every iteration**:\n\n```asm\n.LBB0_6:                          ; =>This Inner Loop Header\n  ...\n  v_readfirstlane_b32 s12, v228   ; lift 16 packed i16 gather indices\n  v_readfirstlane_b32 s13, v229   ; (v228..v235) from VGPR -> SGPR\n  v_readfirstlane_b32 s14, v230\n  v_readfirstlane_b32 s15, v231\n  v_readfirstlane_b32 s60, v232\n  v_readfirstlane_b32 s61, v233\n  v_readfirstlane_b32 s62, v234\n  v_readfirstlane_b32 s63, v235\n  ...\n  tensor_load_to_lds s[8:11], s[36:43], s[12:15], s[60:63]\n  ;                                       ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ row-index operand (SCALAR)\n```\n**8 readfirstlane/iter × ~12 K-iters ≈ 96 dynamic readfirstlanes** per (expert × M-tile).\n\n### Goal of this ticket\n**Eliminate all `v_readfirstlane` inside the K-loop.**\n\n---\n\n## 2. Analysis\n\nThe row indices come from `GatherIndx[(BLOCK_M*block_id + arange) % M]`, an i16 (`uint16`) array. The address is **wave-uniform** (the `X_GATHER_IDX_LAYOUT` slices the 32-lane dimension away, so all lanes hold the same indices) but materialized **per-lane in VGPRs**. Two independent inefficiencies follow.\n\n### 2a. Why `global_load_u16` (1 element/lane) instead of `global_load_b128`?\nThe vector width is `vec = min(128/pointeeBits, getContiguity)`, and `getContiguity = min(align, layout_contigPerThread)` where `align = min(maxMultiple, latticeContig)`. The **layout** supports 16-wide (`contigPerThread=16`), but **`align=1`**:\n- `% M` (runtime `M`) → the `RemSIOp` AxisInfo visitor reports **contiguity 1** and divisibility 1.\n- `GatherIndx += start_m` (runtime `start_m = ExptOffs[expt_id]`) → pointer divisibility = 2 bytes = **1 element**.\n\nSo `vec=1` → 16 separate `global_load_u16`, **wasting 31/32 lanes per load**, plus 8 `v_lshl_or_b32` to pack pairs into `v228..v235`. (LLVM/SLP suggestion: coalesce the `×i16` loads to `×i32`. The ask: make the load **vectorize**.)\n\n### 2b. Why `global_load` instead of `s_load`?\nThis is the **root of the VGPR↔SGPR churn**. The indices are wave-uniform, but Triton emits a per-lane vector load → values land in VGPRs → the TDM gather needs them in SGPRs → `v_readfirstlane` every iteration. If the indices were loaded **straight into SGPRs** (`s_load`) at the top, no VGPR↔SGPR round-trip and no in-loop readfirstlane would be needed.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Exploration\n\n### 3a. Vectorization (kernel-side)\n**What we tried:** make AxisInfo's `align` provable by removing the two collapses from §2a: assert `start_m` is `BLOCK_M`-aligned and drop the `% M` from the address. Patch: [`patches/vectorize_gather_kernel.patch`](patches/vectorize_gather_kernel.patch)\n\n```python\n     start_m = start_m.to(index_type)\n+    start_m = gl.multiple_of(start_m, BLOCK_M)   # provable base alignment\n     pid_n = pid_n.to(index_type)\n     ...\n     offs_x_m = BLOCK_M * block_id + gl.arange(0, BLOCK_M, layout=X_GATHER_IDX_LAYOUT)\n-    offs_x_m = offs_x_m % M\n+    # offs_x_m = offs_x_m % M   # removed to expose contiguity to AxisInfo\n     GatherIndx += start_m\n```\n\n**Why it enables the vectorized load:** with `%M` gone, the offset's `latticeContig` recovers to 128; with `multiple_of(start_m,128)`, the pointer divisibility becomes 16 B (`maxMultiple=8`). Then `align = min(8,128) = 8`, `getContiguity = min(8,16) = 8`, `vec = 8`.\n\n**Codegen after the change** (`upstream/main`, `evidence_upstream/vectorized.amdgcn`):\n| | baseline | vectorized |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| `global_load_u16` | 17 | **1** |\n| `cluster_load_b128` | 0 | **2** |\n| `v_lshl_or_b32` (index pack) | 10 | 2 |\n| `v_readfirstlane` total | 16 | 16 |\n| **`v_readfirstlane` in loop** | 8 | **8** |\n\nSo vectorization fixes the wasted-lane load (`16× u16 + 8 pack → 2× cluster_load_b128`) but **does not touch the in-loop readfirstlane** — the indices still land in VGPRs.\n\n> ⚠️ **Not a production fix.** It passes only for the repro's degenerate `E=1` (`start_m = ExptOffs[0] = 0`, trivially aligned; `M=1024 ≥ 128` makes `%M` an identity). For real multi-expert MoE, `start_m = token_offs_raw[e]` is an arbitrary token boundary (false `multiple_of`) and `%M` does real wrap-masking. This patch is a diagnostic that isolates the AxisInfo gates, not a shippable change.\n\n### 3b. Scalar load (compiler-side)\n**What we tried:** in the AMD backend `LoadOpConversion`, detect a wave-uniform load from the **LinearLayout** (not the SSA `UniformityAnalysis`, which can't see the tensor-layout broadcast and reports the load divergent), readfirstlane the address into SGPRs, and emit an `invariant` `LLVM::LoadOp` so ISel selects `s_load`. The shared GEP base is readfirstlane'd **once** (cached) and only the per-element i32 offset is lifted.\n\nGated by `TRITON_AMD_UNIFORM_SLOAD`. Patch (against **`upstream/main` @ `9c795a`**): [`patches/sload_uniform_main.patch`](patches/sload_uniform_main.patch) — 109 insertions, applies cleanly to upstream; adds `isWaveUniformLoad` (`toLinearLayout(ty).getFreeVariableMasks()[lane]==warpSize-1`), `readFirstLanePtr`, `readFirstLaneInt`, `uniformizeAddr` (peel `extractvalue`→GEP, shared-base cache, sext-peel offset), and a guarded branch in `LoadOpConversion`.\n\n**Build / run on `upstream/main`:** checked out `upstream/main @ 9c795a`, reused the prebuilt LLVM (`62b7cf96`, same pin → no LLVM rebuild), applied the patch (clean), rebuilt `libtriton`, and **ran the a8w4 kernel end-to-end** with `TRITON_AMD_UNIFORM_SLOAD=1`. The kernel runs and is correct (0.0 rel err).\n\n**Codegen with the s_load patch** (`upstream/main`, original kernel, `evidence_upstream/sload.amdgcn`):\n| | baseline | **+ s_load patch** |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| `global_load_u16` (gather) | 16 | **0** (→ `s_load`) |\n| `v_readfirstlane` total | 16 | 16 |\n| **`v_readfirstlane` in loop** | 8 | **0** ✅ |\n| correctness (rel err) | ~0 | **0.0** |\n\nThe in-loop TDM gather now reads its row-index operand straight from SGPRs produced by `s_load`, with **no readfirstlane in the loop**:\n```asm\n; in-loop, no v_readfirstlane:\ntensor_load_to_lds s[16:19], s[36:43], s[4:7], s[8:11]   ; s[4:7],s[8:11] <- s_load result\n```\nAll 16 remaining readfirstlanes are in the **prologue** (lifting the shared base once + the i32 offsets), run once instead of `8 × 12` dynamically.\n\n> Same productionization caveat as a backend feature: `invariant` is only valid for read-only memory (true for `GatherIndx`). A shippable version must prove no aliasing store, or restrict to known read-only kernel inputs.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Comments on LLVM solutions\n\n### 4a. Why LLVM (Machine)LICM cannot hoist the readfirstlane out of the loop\n`v_readfirstlane_b32` lowers from `llvm.amdgcn.readfirstlane`, which is a **convergent** intrinsic. (Machine)LICM does not hoist convergent operations out of loops by default: their result depends on the active-lane mask, which can differ per iteration under divergent control flow, so hoisting is unsound in general — even though here the wave is uniform and the operand (`v228..v235`) is loop-invariant. Compounding it, the prologue's index SGPRs are **reused inside the loop as the TDM descriptor/dest operands** (`tensor_load_to_lds s[36:43], ...`), so register pressure forces the indices to be re-extracted into a fresh SGPR group each iteration. Net: a loop-invariant, convergent op the compiler keeps re-materializing.\n\n### 4b. Can the s_load uniformity be done in LLVM?\n**Partly.** LLVM's AMDGPU backend *will* select `s_load` for a load from a **uniform address** with `!invariant.load` — that is exactly what our patch arranges (readfirstlane the address → SGPR; mark invariant). What LLVM **cannot** do on its own is *discover* that this load is wave-uniform: that fact is a **Triton tensor-layout property** (the load layout's `lane` dimension is free), which is erased once the address is materialized as a **per-lane VGPR** GEP chain (`base + sext((BLOCK_M*block_id + arange) % M)`). LLVM's divergence analysis sees a lane-varying `arange` contribution and conservatively treats the address as divergent. So the uniformity must be injected at the **Triton→LLVM boundary** (where the layout is still known) — either by our explicit readfirstlane+invariant, or by emitting the (uniform) address computation in scalar form so AMDGPU's existing uniform-load selection fires. Pure-LLVM recovery of the lost lane-uniformity is not feasible.\n\n---\n\n## Attachments\n- `patches/vectorize_gather_kernel.patch` — kernel-side vectorization (diagnostic, aiter `moe_a8w4_multicast`).\n- `patches/sload_uniform_main.patch` — compiler-side wave-uniform `s_load` (gated `TRITON_AMD_UNIFORM_SLOAD`), generated against and applies cleanly to Triton `upstream/main @ 9c795a41fc`.\n\n[vectorize_gather_kernel.patch](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/29317047/vectorize_gather_kernel.patch)\n[sload_uniform_main.patch](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/29317050/sload_uniform_main.patch)\n"[m[1;37m,[m
