      [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Great analysis Lixun! As you said, we can use `getFreeVariableMasks` to check whether the load is uniform. Then under the hood, we can switch to emit `s_load` or `s_buffer_load`. For the latter we have direct `@llvm.amdgcn.s.buffer.load.*`; but for the former, from Codex\n\n>  For pointer-based `s_load`, use normal IR:\n> `%v = load i32, ptr addrspace(4) %p, align 4`\n> or, for global memory, make the access uniform and legally scalarizable:\n> ```llvm\n> !0 = !{}\n> %q = getelementptr i32, ptr addrspace(1) %base, i64 %idx, !amdgpu.uniform !0\n> %v = load i32, ptr addrspace(1) %q, align 4, !invariant.load !0\n> ```\n> Use `addrspace(4)` when the data is semantically constant during the kernel dispatch. AMDGPU docs call out that constant address space can use scalar memory instructions because it does not change during kernel execution.\n\nIf it might be hard/unsafe to just totally deduce this inside compiler, maybe we could consider exposing to Gluon as language construct."[m[1;37m,[m
