      [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Nice ticket, thanks @lixzhang_amdeng for elaborating all the aspects and @lzhang20_amdeng's comment. I have a few comments, all consistent with the existing explorations. This is a nice succession to the original gather op enablement feature. I did realize the gathered indices are wave-uniform and it looks quite wasteful - there's no way I can work around it since the gather layout is farther from a natural load-friendly layout. Now I see concretely where that cost gets paid in the MoE kernel.\n\n**The vectorization idea**\nWe could potentially make it vectorize better. One idea I have is masking, load from a previously-aligned address with an off < M mask, then extract the indices that actually become useful. But the boundary handling is tricky: an aligned base means over-reading on the left, and the useful data lands at a dynamic intra-tile offset, so extracting it needs a cross-lane shuffle. Besides, the readfirstlanes will still be there since the data still lands in VGPRs. This only addresses the wasted-lane bandwidth, not the VGPR↔SGPR churn, so it's a hard pass for me.\n\n**The addrspace(4) idea**\nThis looks good on the MoE use case, but to get it working generically the challenge is exactly proving this is read-only. To go down this path we'd have to walk every use, and the hard part is alias analysis - proving there's no write through an alias, not just no direct store. Not sure it's worth swinging that big a hammer.\n\n**The addrspace(1) idea**\nTo be clear how this differs from addrspace(4): they share the read-only requirement, but addrspace(4) bakes it into the pointer type (needs re-typing through constant space), while addrspace(1) keeps the global type and instead attaches metadata (!amdgpu.uniform for uniformity + !invariant.load for no-writes).\n\nThe problem is that this address operand still comes from a per-lane VGPR GEP (base + sext((BLOCK_M*block_id + arange) % M)) that traces back to a lane-varying arange. LLVM's UniformityAnalysis sees that and treats the address as divergent, so we'd be asserting \"uniform\" over a divergent-looking computation — the backend may not reliably scalarize it into s_load.\n\nI like how the patch implements it though: it physically readfirstlane the address into an SGPR so it's genuinely scalar by the time ISel runs. There's no metadata to be honored and no gap between what the IR computes and what we claim, so s_load selection is forced. The !invariant.load it attaches is a separate axis (no-writes), independent of uniformity.\n\n**s_buffer_load**\nAgreed with @lzhang20_amdeng that this is the most robust lowering — feed a scalar offset straight into @llvm.amdgcn.s.buffer.load.*, so there's never a divergent-looking GEP for the backend to trip over and selection is deterministic. The cost is constructing the buffer resource descriptor.\n\nSo my current idea is do it consumer-driven: the gather op is a known scalar sink - walk its index operand back to the producing load and scalarize that load. That turns this from an open-ended alias-analysis project into a bounded, gather-op-specific lowering. I'm planning to land that scoped version with s_buffer_load."[m[1;37m,[m
