      [1;34m"body"[m[1;37m:[m [32m"Discovery summary with an initial prototype: This can work https://github.com/AMD-Triton/triton-mi450/commit/388c7cbb49741f1d3a0bb81d99b3d2a3ca103a7a (`users/jerryyin/moe-gather-sload-shared` branch), but require pile amount of hacks that only works with current AIter kernel. This can easily get disrupted and get hacked on a different kernel.\n\n#### 1. Lane-uniformity is free.\n\nBoth the gather and scatter index verifiers require the index layout to be lane-uniform (they reject any layout that distributes values across lanes). So as long as the trace from the index to the producing load stays layout-preserving (the load carries the same encoding as the index), the load is lane-uniform by construction.\n\n#### 2. Read-only is the hard part — difficult, and close to impossible to prove completely.\n\nWithout a read-only guarantee, the optimization can scalarize a pointer that is actually writable and lower it to s_load / s_buffer_load, causing a silent miscompile: scalar loads read through the scalar (K-)cache, which is not coherent with in-kernel vector stores. If that buffer is written during the kernel, the scalar load returns stale bytes → the gather uses wrong row indices → wrong rows are gathered → silently wrong output, with no crash and no diagnostic (and potentially flaky/data-dependent).\n\nOn top of that, a coarsely written analysis is easy to get wrong: e.g. a function that calls another function which writes the index is easily missed, leading to either a missed opportunity (if we bail conservatively) or a wrong optimization (if we skip the call and wrongly conclude read-only). In-function analysis fundamentally cannot see cross-procedure or cross-argument writes.\n\nSo the robust solution is to attach the LLVM argument properties readonly and noalias, moving read-only from inferred to declared:\n  - **readonly** guarantees the kernel never writes through the index pointer, so the s_load lowering is correct — the scalar cache can't go stale. Otherwise the scalar-cache copy of the index diverges from an in-kernel vector store to the same buffer, and the s_load returns stale indices.\n  - **noalias** prevents the index arg from aliasing another, writable argument — the case a write \"to a different pointer\" secretly lands in the index buffer. Gluon currently has no way to express this, so a malicious (or buggy) kernel writer can intentionally alias a writable arg onto the gather index and silently corrupt it — and, because noalias isn't asserted, the compiler has no way to reject it; the aliasing becomes the caller's undefined behavior only once the contract exists.\n\nBased on this, I'd expose **tt.readonly** and **tt.noalias** properly that resolves correctly to llvm attributes first, then finish the rest of implementation. Because of the scale of changes, I might start with a tentative review first to gather opinions from our fork, then land it incrementally upstream."[m[1;37m,[m
